course: ukrainian-street
lesson: 1
title: Hi, hello and how are you
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Ukrainian has two kinds of hello, just as English has «hiya» for mates and «good afternoon» for the head teacher.
explain: To a friend your own age you say pryVIT (prih-VEET), or the very street zdorOv (zdo-ROV), often with a fist bump.
explain: When your friend Sashko's mum opens the door, you switch to the polite DObryi DEN (good day) — in the morning DObroho RANku, in the evening DObryi VEchir.
explain: Then comes the quick question yak spRAvy? (how's it going?), and the easy answer NOrmalno (fine) — nobody wants a long list.
explain: When you leave the park you shout BUvai or poKA to your friends, the way you'd shout «bye!» or «see ya!».
fact: pryVIT means hi or hello; it is the informal hello for friends, brothers, sisters and kids your age; a grown-up may say it to a kid, but a guest kid greets grown-ups with DObryi DEN; it sounds a little like «private» but has nothing to do with it.
fact: zdorOv is a very casual hiya, short for «be healthy», used between boys and close friends, often with a handshake or a fist bump — never to a granny or a teacher.
fact: DObryi DEN means good day; it is the safe polite hello to a friend's mum, dad, granny, a neighbour or a shopkeeper from late morning on.
fact: DObroho RANku means good morning, for example to the friend's family at breakfast after a sleepover; RANku has nothing to do with the English word «rank».
fact: DObryi VEchir means good evening, said to adults when you come over in the evening.
fact: zdrAstuite is a polite hello that some older people use; it can sound old-fashioned or Russian to some, and many Ukrainian families now prefer DObryi DEN, which is always a fine answer.
fact: yak spRAvy? means how are you or how's it going; it is a quick hello question, not a request for a long report.
fact: yak ty? asks one friend how he is, using ty, the close «you»; to an elder or to several people you say yak vy?
fact: vy is both «you all» and the polite «you» to one grown-up, so yak vy? suits the friend's mum or granny.
fact: NOrmalno means fine or okay, and DObre means good; it is the everyday answer to «how are you?», often followed by a ty? (and you?), or a vy? to a grown-up.
fact: BUvai is an informal bye to friends; to a granny you would say do pobAchennya instead.
fact: poKA means bye or see ya between kids; on its own it can also mean «for now» or «while».
exercise: What does pryVIT mean? | Hi, hello (informal) | Private, keep out | Good evening (polite) | Goodbye for ever
exercise: Who do you greet with pryVIT? | A friend or a kid your own age | The friend's granny, at the door | The head teacher | A shopkeeper you don't know
exercise: What kind of hello is zdorOv? | A very casual hiya between boys and close friends | A formal hello for teachers | A hello used only on birthdays | A polite hello used for grandparents and teachers
exercise: zdorOv comes from a wish. Which one? | Be healthy | Be quiet | Be on time | Be quick
exercise: Which hello is safest to say to your friend's mum at lunchtime? | DObryi DEN | zdorOv | pryVIT-pryVIT | poKA
exercise: When do you say DObroho RANku? | In the morning, for example at breakfast | Late in the evening, just before bed time | When you leave somebody's house for the night | Only on someone's birthday, at the party
exercise: What does DObryi VEchir mean? | Good evening | Good morning | Good night, sleep well | Goodbye, see you
exercise: An old neighbour says zdrAstuite to you. What is a fine answer? | DObryi DEN | zdorOv | BUvai | poKA
exercise: Why do many Ukrainian families prefer DObryi DEN to zdrAstuite? | zdrAstuite can sound old-fashioned or Russian to some | zdrAstuite is a rude swear word that adults use when they're cross | zdrAstuite means goodbye | zdrAstuite is only for babies
exercise: What does yak spRAvy? mean? | How's it going? | What's your name? | Where do you live? | What time is it?
exercise: What answer is expected to yak spRAvy? | A short NOrmalno or DObre | A long list of your whole day | Your age and address | Silence and a nod
exercise: Who do you ask yak ty? | One friend your own age | The friend's granny | A whole class of children | A teacher you have just met
exercise: Why is yak vy? right for the friend's mum? | vy is the polite «you» to a grown-up | vy is just the Ukrainian word for «mum» | vy is only ever used when talking to girls | vy is the word for «tired» or «sleepy»
exercise: What does NOrmalno mean? | Fine, okay | Strange, odd | Not very good | Normal-sized
exercise: What does BUvai mean? | Bye (informal) | Come in, please | Thank you very much | Good morning to you
exercise: Who should NOT get a BUvai when you leave? | The friend's granny | Your best friend | Sashko's little brother | Kids in the park
exercise: What does poKA mean between kids? | Bye, see ya | Wait a moment | Pass the ball | Hello again
test: «pryVIT means private, it's like keep out» Which is right? | No, pryVIT means hi, it only sounds a bit like «private» | Yes, it is written on doors | Yes, it is how you say «secret» | No, it means good evening
test: Your mate Dmytryk says «zdorOv!» and holds out his fist. What fits best? | Bump his fist and say zdorOv! or pryVIT! | Say do pobAchennya to him and give a little bow | Ask him if he feels ill | Say DObryi VEchir, pane
test: «just say zdorOv to his nan, it's friendly innit» What is right? | No, zdorOv is the street hello; say DObryi DEN to her | Yes, grannies like the street hello best, it makes them feel young | Yes, it means good morning | No, you should say poKA to her
test: You arrive for a sleepover at 7 in the evening and Sashko's dad opens the door. What do you say? | DObryi VEchir | DObroho RANku | BUvai | zdorOv
test: Next morning at breakfast, what do you say to the family? | DObroho RANku | DObryi VEchir | DObroi NOchi | poKA
test: Sashko's aunt asks «yak spRAvy?». You answer with five minutes about your football match. What happens? | Nothing bad, but a quick NOrmalno, a vy? was all she expected | She thinks you didn't understand the question | It is very rude in Ukraine to answer at all | She wanted your age, not your news
test: What would happen if you asked the friend's granny «yak ty?»? | It would sound too familiar; yak vy? is polite to her | Nothing at all, because ty is the polite «you» for older people | She would think you asked her name | She would answer with the time
test: «normalno means bad, like meh» Which is right? | No, NOrmalno means fine or okay | Yes, it means bad | Yes, it means very sad | No, it means excellent and amazing
test: In a cartoon a boy says «poKA!» as he arrives at a party. What is wrong? | poKA is bye, not hello | Nothing, poKA is hello | poKA is only for grown-ups | poKA means thank you
test: Sashko's mum says «pryVIT» to you. Why is that fine, while you should not say it to her? | Adults may be informal to kids, but a guest kid greets adults politely | pryVIT is only for adults | pryVIT means good day for adults | She made a mistake, pryVIT is rude
test: «shut up with yak spRAvy, just say hi» What does the grumpy kid want? | Just a short pryVIT, no «how are you» | A long answer about your day and your week | Your full name, your age and where you live | A polite DObryi DEN, as if to a teacher
exam: Identify the greeting appropriate to be addressed by a visiting child to the mother of his companion during the daytime. | DObryi DEN | zdorOv | pryVIT | poKA
exam: Select the response consistent with the established usage of the expression zdorOv. | It is a highly informal greeting exchanged between boys and close companions | It is a formal greeting reserved for teachers | It is a valediction employed at night | It is an enquiry as to a person's health addressed to physicians
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the distinction between yak ty? and yak vy? | The former is addressed to a single familiar person, whereas the latter is addressed to an elder or to several persons | The former is polite, whereas the latter is informal | Both are reserved exclusively for grandparents | The former concerns health, whereas the latter concerns the weather
exam: Ascertain the customary reply to the enquiry yak spRAvy? | A brief answer such as NOrmalno, possibly followed by a reciprocal enquiry | An exhaustive account of the day's events | A statement of one's age and address | A formal valediction
